<<<
If the writer wishes to keep his thats, he must correct had authorized into had not authorized, & knew into did not know; the repetition of t. has lulled him into the state in which yes & no mean the same thing:—The Minister added
===
If the writer wishes to keep his *that*s, he must correct *had authorized* into *had not authorized,* & *knew* into *did not know*; the repetition of *t.* has lulled him into the state in which *yes* & *no* mean the same thing:—*The Minister added*
>>>

<<<
that there was no need to say that the Government knew nothing about these statements, still less [that it] had authorized them, or [that it] knew what amount of truth there might be in them.
===
*that there was no need to say that the Government knew nothing about these statements, still less* [*that it*] *had authorized them, or* [*that it*] *knew what amount of truth there might be in them.*
